<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Sudden versus gradual</h2>
<p>Most homeowner policies cover sudden and accidental water damage, such as a burst supply line or a failed water heater, and exclude long-term seepage, flood and surface water. Many disputes start with the insurer arguing a leak was gradual. If it accepts the loss but underpays it, appraisal can settle the amount. If it denies the claim as gradual, that is a coverage question.</p>
<h2>Where water estimates fall short</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Drying left incomplete.</strong> Equipment pulled before materials reached dry standard, or no drying logs at all.</li>
<li><strong>Tear-out understated.</strong> Wet insulation, baseboards, cabinets and subfloor that should have come out but were scoped to stay.</li>
<li><strong>Continuous finishes.</strong> Flooring, paint and ceiling texture that run through adjoining rooms and can’t be repaired in one spot.</li>
<li><strong>Crawlspaces.</strong> Many Oregon homes sit over crawlspaces where water collects unseen: vapor barrier, insulation and framing damage get missed.</li>
<li><strong>Mold remediation</strong> limited by policy sublimits, or left out entirely when growth started because the drying was slow.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Flood is a separate policy</h2>
<p>River flooding along the Willamette, Umpqua, Coquille and other rivers, and coastal flooding, are generally excluded from homeowner policies and covered, if at all, under a separate flood policy. Flood policies have their own claim and appeal rules. We focus on water losses under property policies, and we will tell you if your loss looks like a flood claim.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Documentation that helps</b>Moisture readings, drying logs, photos of wet materials before removal, and the plumber’s invoice describing the failure.</div>
<h2>Condos and multi-unit buildings</h2>
<p>A leak in one unit can damage several below it. We help sort out which policy responds to which damage and scope the repairs consistently across units.</p>

<h2>Questions about water damage claim appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>The insurer says my leak was long-term. What now?</summary><div class="a"><p>If it denied the claim on that basis, it is a coverage dispute. If it paid part and says the rest was long-term, appraisal may help value the covered portion. Send us the letter.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is mold covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies limit or exclude mold, often with a sublimit for mold that results from a covered water loss. The wording matters.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do I prove water damage after the repairs?</summary><div class="a"><p>Keep the drying logs, moisture maps, photos and invoices from the mitigation company. Ask for them if you don’t have them.</p></div></details></div>
